 see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq 1.239) = 0.990.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.239.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161734 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p$, where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k)$. Given that $X \\sim \\chi^2(7)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\leq x) = 0.005$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |" 13194139534722 17592186045417 false] [17592186161734 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$ Given that $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\leq x) = 0.005.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161734 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x)$. So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X > x) &= 1 - P(X \\le x) \\\\\n&= 1 - 0.005 \\\\\n&= 0.995\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7)$, we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7$.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989$." 13194139534722 17592186045417 false] [17592186161734 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X > x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.005 \\\\  & =0.995\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X \\sim \\chi^2(7),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=7.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X > 0.989) = 0.995.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.989.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161735 1071 "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2\n$$\n\nwhere $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 10.$ Find the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p,$ where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k).$*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161735 1071 "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n$Y = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2$\nwhere $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i\\leq 10$. Find the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99$.\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\geq x) = p$, where $X\\sim \\chi^2(k)$.*\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |" 13194139534722 17592186045417 false] [17592186161735 1074 "If $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for\n\n$$\n1\\leq i \\leq k\n$$\n\nare independent normal random variables, then the sum\n$Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{k}^2$\nfollows a $\\chi^2(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2 \\sim \\chi^2(10)\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq y){:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Y \\le y) &= 1 - P(Y \\ge y) \\\\\n0.99 &= 1 - P(Y \\ge y) \\\\\nP(Y \\ge y) &= 0.01\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq y) = 0.01\n$$\n\nThis can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq 23.209) = 0.01\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq 23.209) = 0.99\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $y$ that satisfies\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq y) = 0.99\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\ny=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\n$$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 false] [17592186161735 1074 "If $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$ for $1\\leq i \\leq k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{k}^2\n$$\n\nfollows a $\\chi^2(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY = Z_1^2+Z_2^2+\\cdots + Z_{10}^2 \\sim \\chi^2(10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\geq y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.99 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\geq y) = 0.01$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,20.090\\,$ | $\\,15.507\\,$ | $\\,13.362\\,$ | $\\,12.027\\,$ | $\\,11.030\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\geq 23.209) = 0.01\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\leq 23.209) = 0.99.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\leq y) = 0.99$ is $y=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}23.209}.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434097 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434098 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434094 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434096 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434093 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186161926 1038 #edb/ref 17592186434095 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186162177 1050 600.0 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186162238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186162238 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X \\ge x) = p$, where $X∼χ^{2}(k)$. Given that the random variable $X∼χ^{2}(5)$, find the value of $x$ such that $P(X \\ge x) = 0.99$.\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $3$ | $0.072$ | $0.115$ | $0.216$ | $0.352$ | $0.584$ |\n| $4$ | $0.207$ | $0.297$ | $0.484$ | $0.711$ | $1.064$ |\n| $5$ |