\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{25}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(25).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.1$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $25$ | $\\,44.314\\,$ | $\\,37.652\\,$ | $\\,34.382\\,$ | $\\,32.282\\,$ | $\\,30.675\\,$ |\n| $26$ | $\\,45.642\\,$ | $\\,38.885\\,$ | $\\,35.563\\,$ | $\\,33.429\\,$ | $\\,31.795\\,$ |\n| $27$ | $\\,46.963\\,$ | $\\,40.113\\,$ | $\\,36.741\\,$ | $\\,34.574\\,$ | $\\,32.912\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 34.382)=0.1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=34.382.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434094 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434088 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434094 1069 "q-108266" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434094 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434094 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$  Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X>x)=0.950.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $\\,0.676\\,$ | $\\,0.872\\,$ | $\\,1.237\\,$ | $\\,1.635\\,$ | $\\,2.204\\,$ |\n| $7$ | $\\,0.989\\,$ | $\\,1.239\\,$ | $\\,1.690\\,$ | $\\,2.167\\,$ | $\\,2.833\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434094 1072 #uuid "4082c7b9-656c-5af0-a7d3-4e13cbac6782" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434094 1074 "Since $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=8.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.344\\,$ | $\\,1.646\\,$ | $\\,2.180\\,$ | $\\,2.733\\,$ | $\\,3.490\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>2.733)=0.950.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.733.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434095 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434092 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434095 1069 "q-252038" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434095 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434095 1071 "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{40}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 40.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $39$ | $\\,62.428\\,$ | $\\,54.572\\,$ | $\\,50.660\\,$ | $\\,48.126\\,$ | $\\,46.173\\,$ |\n| $40$ | $\\,63.691\\,$ | $\\,55.758\\,$ | $\\,51.805\\,$ | $\\,49.244\\,$ | $\\,47.269\\,$ |\n| $41$ | $\\,64.950\\,$ | $\\,56.942\\,$ | $\\,52.949\\,$ | $\\,50.360\\,$ | $\\,48.363\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434095 1072 #uuid "6afc0dfa-f42f-5b87-9c8b-8fbf362ef997" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434095 1074 "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{40}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(40).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.15$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $39$ | $\\,62.428\\,$ | $\\,54.572\\,$ | $\\,50.660\\,$ | $\\,48.126\\,$ | $\\,46.173\\,$ |\n| $40$ | $\\,63.691\\,$ | $\\,55.758\\,$ | $\\,51.805\\,$ | $\\,49.244\\,$ | $\\,47.269\\,$ |\n| $41$ | $\\,64.950\\,$ | $\\,56.942\\,$ | $\\,52.949\\,$ | $\\,50.360\\,$ | $\\,48.363\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 49.244)=0.15.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=49.244.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434096 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434087 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434096 1069 "q-108300" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434096 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434096 1071 "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$ Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(X\\le x)=0.025.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $\\,0.072\\,$ | $\\,0.115\\,$ | $\\,0.216\\,$ | $\\,0.352\\,$ | $\\,0.584\\,$ |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n| $5$ | $\\,0.412\\,$ | $\\,0.554\\,$ | $\\,0.831\\,$ | $\\,1.145\\,$ | $\\,1.610\\,$ |" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434096 1072 #uuid "824977e3-712a-57cf-beb4-b57ff8f0ad52" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434096 1074 "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>x) & =1-P(X\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.025 \\\\  & =0.975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{χ}^{2}(4),$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=4.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.995$ | $0.990$ | $0.975$ | $0.950$ | $0.900$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $\\,0.207\\,$ | $\\,0.297\\,$ | $\\,0.484\\,$ | $\\,0.711\\,$ | $\\,1.064\\,$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X>0.484)=0.975.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=0.484.$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434097 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434089 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434097 1069 "q-108162" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434097 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434097 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ calculate $P(X<6).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x≈33.86574\n$$\n\n*The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434097 1072 #uuid "8899331b-91a4-5c31-88b7-72adb8fd73b2" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434097 1074 "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\frac{1}{96}\\cdot 33.86574 \\\\  & ≈0.3528\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186434091 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434098 1069 "q-108186" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434098 1071 "Given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8),$ what is $P(X<6)$ expressed as an integral?\n\n*Hint: The probability density function ${χ}^{2}(k)$ is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434098 1072 #uuid "cdd1fbde-a478-5539-a14c-9c15409e9daa" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true] [17592186434098 1074 "A chi-square random variable $X∼{χ}^{2}(k)$ with $k$ degrees of freedom has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{2}^{k/2}Γ(\\frac{k}{2})}{x}^{k/2-1}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $X∼{χ}^{2}(8).$ So, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16Γ(4)}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecalling that $Γ(n)=(n-1)!$ for any natural number $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\nΓ(4)=3!=6.\n$$\n\nSo, the probability distribution becomes\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $P(X<6)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<6) & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{96}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{96}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{x}^{3}{e}^{-x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534722 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0823d426-5b2c-5ac6-b64a-d3179098eb29" 17592186434065 "answer-0fb991ce-4b7c-5de9-ab03-280608b86db1" 17592186434066 "answer-2454fa59-6173-5a32-9c1b-31dc5e951511" 17592186434067 "answer-29c35df3-8e92-5fc7-a88c-ae44748bd3b0" 17592186434068 "answer-2efa2847-8c8c-50f1-b144-1c847e19fa35" 17592186434069 "answer-36a9fc04-92a0-545c-a9b1-c789f48da005" 17592186434070 "answer-37aa76aa-7b1